Exit code: 0
---
Stdout:
ST108  Response violates schema                                             HIGH
checks response_schema_conformance, response_headers_conformance   WFC 200

A response body or header holds a value that its documented schema rejects.

Why it matters
  Generated clients and typed SDKs trust the schema. A `null` where the schema
  promises a string becomes a crash in the consumer's code, far from your API,
  with no hint of the cause. Every consumer who wrote code against the
  documented shape carries that bug.

Example
  schema:
    openapi: 3.0.3
    paths:
      /users/me:
        get:
          responses:
            '200':
              description: OK
              content:
                application/json:
                  schema:
                    type: object
                    properties:
                      email:
                        type: string
  response:
    HTTP/1.1 200 OK
    Content-Type: application/json

    {"email": null}

How Schemathesis found it
  Schemathesis validates the body against the schema for the response status and
  media type, and each documented header against its schema. The title names the
  part: `Response violates schema`, `Response header does not conform to the
  schema`, or an `SSE ...` title for Server-Sent Events.

  In the failure output for a body:

  - The first line names the violation, such as `null is not of type "string"`.
  - `Validated against the response schema for status code 200.` names the
    response definition. A `default` or range match names that key instead.
  - `Schema at /properties/email:` gives the location in the schema and the
    subschema that failed.
  - `Value:` shows what the API returned.

How to fix
  Decide which side is wrong.

  - The API is wrong: fix the serializer or the data. A `null` points to a field
    your storage allows to be empty.
  - The schema is wrong: change it to match, for example `nullable: true` in
    OpenAPI 3.0. Widening the schema weakens what clients can rely on.

  Too many `format` failures in bodies: set `validate-formats = false` under
  `[checks.response_schema_conformance]`. Body format violations then go
  unreported, so turn it back on once you fix the structural failures. The
  setting leaves header formats checked.

Verify the fix
  Run `st replay <case id>` with the ID from the `Test Case ID:` line of the
  failure output. It reruns the stored case and reports whether it still fails;
  once it passes, replay deletes the stored case. The replay needs the run's
  credentials: run it with the same config, or pass `--header`, `--auth` or
  `--auth-wfc`.

Turn it off
  `--exclude-checks response_schema_conformance,response_headers_conformance`
  or in schemathesis.toml:
    [checks.response_schema_conformance]
    enabled = false
    [checks.response_headers_conformance]
    enabled = false
  Also disables:
    ST104  Missing Content-Type header
    ST107  Missing required headers
    ST109  JSON deserialization error
    ST110  Content deserialization error
    ST111  Discriminator value not in schema mapping

Docs  https://schemathesis.readthedocs.io/en/stable/reference/failures/#st108
